summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Diabetes and hypertension.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bevley is a 24-year-old female who presents today for her yearly exam. She reports she has been doing well and started a new job at a bakery. She has been snacking a lot during her shifts and eating a lot of desserts. She has been having trouble controlling her blood sugars.

The patient reports her blood sugars have been elevated. She has been taking her medication as prescribed, but her diet has been poor. She denies missing any doses. She reports her blood pressure has been good, and she has been sleeping well. She denies any concerns about hurting herself.

The patient denies any other symptoms.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, weight loss, or weight gain.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, cough, or wheezing.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or weakness.
• Neurological: Denies headaches, weakness, or numbness/tingling.
• Skin: Denies rashes or lesions.
• Endocrine: Denies hot flashes or cold intolerance.

MEDICATIONS

• Metformin 2 times a day

VITALS

Blood pressure: Normal
Weight: 130 lbs
Height: 5'6"

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Grade 2 systolic ejection murmur.
• Musculoskeletal: No edema. Equal bilateral pedal pulses.
• Neurological: No carotid bruits or thyromegaly. Neurovascular intact.

ASSESSMENT

1. Diabetes.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports her blood sugars have been elevated. Her hemoglobin A1c is elevated as well, indicating that her blood sugars have been out of control. She is currently taking metformin 2 times a day. She denies missing any doses. She reports her blood pressure has been good, and she has been sleeping well. She denies any concerns about hurting herself. We have reviewed her diet and she reports it has been poor.
• Patient Education and Counseling: The patient understands that her diet has been poor and that this is the cause of her elevated blood sugars. We discussed the importance of diet control in managing her diabetes. I recommended she try to cut down on snacking and additional sweets. I also recommended that she check her blood sugars in the morning and call them in in 2 weeks so we can see how her blood sugars are doing. If they are not under control, we will discuss adding another medication.
2. Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports her blood pressure has been good. She is currently on lisinopril 10 mg once a day. Her blood pressure is stable.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to continue taking her current medication.

PLAN

• Diabetes: Continue metformin 2 times a day. I recommended she try to cut down on snacking and additional sweets. I recommended that she check her blood sugars in the morning and call them in in 2 weeks so we can see how her blood sugars are doing. If they are not under control, we will discuss adding another medication.
• Hypertension: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once a day.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>
