Abnormal lab finding.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Michelle Smith is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal lab findings. She reports that she was out hiking in the woods and noticed a tick on her body. She denies any other symptoms. She states that she has been feeling tired, lacking energy, swollen joints in her elbows, and body aches. She has also been experiencing nausea and has been unable to eat much. She has been trying to keep down toast and crackers. She denies fever. She reports that she has had a rash elsewhere on her body that looks like a bull's-eye. She denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and abdominal pain. She has been monitoring her blood sugars at home and notes that they have been okay. She is still taking metformin. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. She states that she is taking lisinopril and has been keeping her blood pressure under control.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Reports fatigue, lack of energy, and body aches.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports joint pain in her elbows.
• Neurological: Denies headache, fever, or chills.
• Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting.
• Skin: Reports rash.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Constitutional: In no acute distress.
• Musculoskeletal: Swelling of the bilateral elbow joints. Pain with palpation of both elbow joints.
• Skin: Warm to palpation of the bull's-eye rash on her abdomen on the left-hand side. Non-tender to palpation. No evidence of cellulitis.

RESULTS

• Lyme titer: Elevated.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Ms. Smith is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal lab findings. She reports symptoms consistent with Lyme disease, including fatigue, lack of energy, joint pain in her elbows, and body aches. She also reports nausea, vomiting, and rash. Her vital signs are within normal limits. Her physical exam is significant for swelling of the bilateral elbow joints and a bull's-eye rash on her abdomen on the left-hand side. She has an elevated Lyme titer. We will order further testing to confirm the diagnosis.

Ms. Smith will continue to take doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for three weeks. We will follow up with her in one week. We will also order a hemoglobin A1c to ensure that she does not need any changes to her metformin. For her hypertension, we will continue her lisinopril 10 mg daily. She will continue to record her blood pressure regularly. We will order a lipid panel to ensure that she is doing well. }}<|eot_id|>
