summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

High blood pressure.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for a follow-up of hypertension and palpitations.

The patient reports that he was recently seen in the emergency room with high blood pressure and palpitations. He states that he was having some coffee and no breakfast at that time, and he believes that was a panic attack rather than a hypertensive emergency. He denies having palpitations since that time.

The patient reports that he has a history of hypertension. He denies a family history of hypertension. He states that he was recently placed on Benicar HCT, 40-25, and he is also taking Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He reports that he is checking his blood pressure daily at home and his readings are between 146-155.

The patient reports that he is trying to cut back on his salt intake. He states that he has been walking on the treadmill when he can. He denies any other medical problems.

MEDICATIONS

• Benicar HCT, 40-25
• Amlodipine, 10 mg daily

VITALS

Blood pressure: 146/55 mmHg

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Auscultation of the heart reveals a regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

RESULTS

Thyroid profile from 3/1/2021: Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) 2.1 mcU/mL. Free thyroxine (FT4) 1.2 ng/dL.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Resistant hypertension.

The patient is a 49-year-old male who presents with hypertension. He is currently taking three medications and has not achieved adequate blood pressure control. He is also taking Benicar HCT, 40-25, and Amlodipine, 10 mg daily. His blood pressure is currently running 146/55 mmHg. He reports that he is checking his blood pressure daily at home and his readings are between 146-155. He denies a family history of hypertension. He is trying to cut back on his salt intake and has been walking on the treadmill when he can.

I have added Spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in two weeks. If he is not controlled on Spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to resistant hypertension clinic. He was advised of the potential side effects of Spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. He was also advised of the potential side effects of Amlodipine, including erectile dysfunction. He was advised of the importance of reducing his sodium intake and increasing his potassium intake. He was also advised to review the dash eating plan at heart.org.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will see the patient in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
