Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up of adult hydrocephalus. He was initially seen in May 2020 for an 8-10 year history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. The patient underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed. The shunt was set at 2.0.

The patient reports that he has been doing much better since his surgery. He notes that his walking has improved and he is now able to walk to the dining room. He is still having some issues with bladder control, but he has leveled off. He is wearing a diaper and has not had any issues with sores. He has not had any pain or headaches since his surgery.

The patient's daughter reports that she has noticed an improvement in his walking. He is able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which he was unable to do prior to his surgery. She also reports that he is doing better with his bladder control, but has leveled off. She notes that she has to help him at times.

The patient reports that he is doing better cognitively. He states that he is able to have conversations without feeling lost.

The patient reports no pain or discomfort where the shunt is located.

