Right knee pain.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. Last seen in clinic 2 months ago for right knee pain. He received a steroid injection at that time. He reports that the injection provided temporary relief for a couple of weeks, but his pain has returned. He is now experiencing pain in his entire leg, which is swollen.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful in alleviating his symptoms. He reports that he has been slowed down a little bit and has had to slow down activities. He has been using a cane to help him get up from a sitting position.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that he is taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Neurological: Denies headaches.
Skin: Denies rashes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the right knee reveals slight diminished range of motion. There is pitting around the knee and ankle. Capillary refill is normal in the toes. The knee is warm to touch.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Rheumatoid Arthritis.
Right Knee Pain.
Right Leg Swelling.

Patient is a 71-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for ongoing management of his rheumatoid arthritis. He reports that his right knee pain has returned after a steroid injection 2 months ago. He is now experiencing pain in his entire leg, which is swollen. The patient reports that he has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful in alleviating his symptoms. He reports that he has been slowed down a little bit and has had to slow down activities. He has been using a cane to help him get up from a sitting position.

Medical Reasoning: The patient's symptoms are consistent with right knee pain and right leg swelling. The patient's right knee pain returned after a steroid injection 2 months ago. The patient's right knee pain is likely related to his rheumatoid arthritis. The patient's right leg swelling is likely related to right knee pain and right knee swelling.

Patient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to continue with his current medications and to follow up with his PCP. He was advised to try compression socks, reduce his salt intake, and prop his feet up at night. He was advised to make an appointment with his PCP for blood work in the future.

Medical Treatment: The patient received a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection today. The risks and benefits and alternatives were explained. The patient was advised of the risks of the steroid injection, including, but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
