summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Rash on face.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Ms. Miller is a 42-year-old female who presents for follow-up of her perioral dermatitis. She reports that she is doing well and her rash is better. She has been using doxycycline once daily and states she has noticed some plateau in her improvement. She has also been using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily. She denies any other rashes.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports she is not breastfeeding at this time.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports she is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, the metro cream, and doxycycline. She also reports she is not using any other lotions.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers.
• Dermatologic: Reports a rash on her face.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or difficulty breathing.
• Skin: Reports a rash on her face.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few faint erythematous papules and a little bit of redness around and underneath the nares.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient presents with perioral dermatitis. She is currently using doxycycline once daily and has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily. She reports a plateau in her improvement and is interested in using the elidel cream. She is not currently using any other lotions.

Patient Education and Counseling

Patient understands that the doxycycline is a low-risk medication. I advised her to apply the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash resolves, then once daily for an additional week before stopping. She will continue to use the face wash. She will continue to use the metro cream. She understands that she may continue to use the face wash and metro cream once the rash resolves.

Patient Agreements

The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

1. Apply the tacrolimus ointment to the rash on her face twice daily until the rash resolves, then once daily for an additional week before stopping.
2. Continue to use the face wash.
3. Continue to use the metro cream.
4. If the rash is getting worse rather than better, contact the clinic.
5. If she is starting to get dry, reduce the frequency of the face wash to once a day.
6. Hold off until the next visit before starting back any of her old products. If she is starting to flair again, she can try the tacrolimus ointment for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then restart the doxycycline as well.

INSTRUCTIONS TO THE PHARMACY

Cancel the elidel cream. }}<|eot_id|>
