The patient is a 67-year-old male who presents today with right shoulder pain. He reports that he was playing tackle football and got tackled and landed on his right shoulder. He denies numbness or tingling in his fingers. He denies any problems with the strength of his arm. He is right handed. He reports that he has had an appendectomy 3-4 years ago. The patient is in no acute distress. On examination, he has pain to palpation over the AC joint. He has decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. He has pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. He has a palpable radial pulse. X-ray of the right shoulder shows an acute AC joint fracture.

