summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Noah Smith is a 55-year-old male with a history of diabetes, presents today for evaluation of low back pain. The pain started approximately a week ago. He denies any injury. The patient states that he had pain in the back, which went away. He thought that was the end of it, but after a few hours, the pain came back. The pain is in the middle of the back, possibly on the left side. He states that it sometimes goes to the left eye, but it is worse with sudden movements to the left. The pain is constant, and he is unable to twist or bend.

The patient states that he has had diabetes for a while. He is on metformin twice a day. He denies any recent blood sugar checks. He states that he is trying to exercise and watch his diet. He does occasionally eat sweets, but he tries to limit them.

The patient denies any numbness or tingling in his feet.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or nausea.
Neurological: Denies headache or dizziness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the skin is normal. Examination of the eyes is normal. Examination of the ears is normal. Examination of the respiratory system is normal. Examination of the cardiovascular system is normal. Examination of the musculoskeletal system is normal. Examination of the lower extremities is normal.

RESULTS

X-rays of the low back ordered.

ASSESSMENT

1. Musculoskeletal low back strain.
2. Diabetes.

PLAN

For the low back pain, I am going to order a low back x-ray. I do not expect to see anything significant, but it will tell me how the joint spaces look and if we are dealing with any disk issues. I am also going to order physical therapy to help with the pain and strengthen some of the muscles in the back.
For the diabetes, my impression is that the diabetes is under control. I noticed that we have not had an A1c for almost 5 months. I am going to order an A1c. I want the patient to continue with his diet and exercise. He will continue with his metformin. I will not be making any changes today, but we will look at that once I get the results of the A1c.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will take 400 mg of ibuprofen. He can take that occasionally with some Tylenol when the pain gets more severe, up to 3 times a day. He will take one Flexeril once a day in the evening. He will be careful if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking those.

I will refer the patient to a pain doctor if we are still having significant pain after we do the x-ray.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
