summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Smith is a pleasant 56-year-old male who presents today with right knee pain. The patient states he injured his right knee 2.5 weeks ago when he stepped off a curb. The patient denies falling down. He states that he initially felt pain in his ankle, but after the ankle pain went away, he noticed that his knee was sore. The patient notes that it clicks when he walks. The pain is worse when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient denies any pain at rest. He has been taking ibuprofen, but notes that it does not help with the pain during the day. The patient states that at night, it does help him sleep better. The patient denies any numbness or tingling in his foot.

The patient states that he is active and rides a bike. He notes that he does squats and that is when he feels the most clicking. He denies any pain with bending. The patient states that he has broken his second toe on his right foot three times in the last 10 years. He notes that he has some numbness depending on the shoe he wears.

The patient denies any radicular symptoms.

MEDICAL REASON

The patient presents today with right knee pain. He reports a 2.5-week history of right knee pain after stepping off a curb. He denies any fall. He reports that he initially felt pain in his ankle, but after the ankle pain went away, he noticed that his knee was sore. He notes that it clicks when he walks. The patient denies any pain at rest. He has been taking ibuprofen, but notes that it does not help with the pain during the day. He states that at night, it does help him sleep better. He denies any numbness or tingling in his foot. The patient reports that he is active and rides a bike. He notes that he does squats and that is when he feels the most clicking. He denies any pain with bending. The patient states that he has broken his second toe on his right foot three times in the last 10 years. He notes that he has some numbness depending on the shoe he wears. The patient denies any radicular symptoms. He reports that his symptoms have remained the same for the past 2 weeks. He states that the only time his symptoms get worse is when he is exerting himself doing up and down movements outside.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee reveals slight edema. There is no pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. The patient has a positive varus test. There is full range of motion. There is a negative Lachman sign.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee was reviewed and it does not show any acute fracture or bony abnormality.

ASSESSMENT

Right lateral collateral ligament strain.

PLAN

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. I will refer him to physical therapy. I will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I advised him to ice his knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off 4 times a day. If his symptoms do not improve, we will consider an MRI.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient was advised to ice his knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off 4 times a day. }}<|eot_id|>
