summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Nonhealing foot ulcer.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Diabetes.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports she will be walking around at the zoo this weekend.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK
- Right foot: 1x2 cm circular wound on dorsal aspect of lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. Yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. No evidence of fluid collection. No necrosis. No bony exposure. No pain to palpation.

RESULTS

X-ray of right foot: No evidence of osteomyelitis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Diabetic foot ulcer.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a 1x2 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. She has a history of type 2 diabetes. The wound has been present for approximately 1 year. The patient reports she has had some drainage and increased fatigue. She has been on keflex for 5 months.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the importance of controlling her diabetes with the patient. I recommended that she get back with her PCP to discuss the elevated A1c level. I recommended that she wear a surgical shoe to take pressure off the area.
- Medical Treatment: I will order an ABI. I performed a debridement of the ulcer in the office today. I prescribed Colaganase ointment to be applied to the wound once a day and covered with a sterile dressing. I will see her back in 2 weeks to repeat the debridement and assess the wound.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
