summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Chest pain.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient is a pleasant and cooperative 68-year-old female who presents today for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where we had to place another stent. The patient reports that she had been doing well since her last heart attack, but she had been experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath. She states that she had been walking her dog every day, but that she had been noticing some leg swelling. The patient reports that she is able to lie flat at night, but that she has been having to sit up a little bit. She states that she has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She notes that she has been less strict with her diet since the last heart attack and that she loves pizza. She states that she has been trying to watch her salt intake, but that it is difficult.

MEDICATIONS

Patient is currently taking aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, toprol 50 mg daily, and metformin.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.
Musculoskeletal: Denies leg swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neck: Supple without JVD.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Ejection systolic murmur 3/6 at the left base.
Musculoskeletal: 1+ pitting edema of the right lower extremities.

RESULTS

EKG: Normal sinus rhythm with a good R wave progression and evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Coronary artery disease.
Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where we had to place another stent. The patient reports that she had been doing well since her last heart attack, but that she had been experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath. She states that she had been walking her dog every day, but that she had been noticing some leg swelling. The patient reports that she is able to lie flat at night, but that she has been having to sit up a little bit. She states that she has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She notes that she has been less strict with her diet since the last heart attack and that she loves pizza. She states that she has been trying to watch her salt intake, but that it is difficult.
Medical Treatment: The patient will continue her aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, toprol 50 mg daily, and metformin.
Patient Education and Counseling: I provided the patient with a diet sheet and discussed the importance of watching her salt intake. I recommended that she avoid preserved meats like pepperoni and try some alternatives.
2. Reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.
Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where we had to place another stent. The patient reports that she had been doing well since her last heart attack, but that she had been experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath. She states that she had been walking her dog every day, but that she had been noticing some leg swelling. The patient reports that she is able to lie flat at night, but that she has been having to sit up a little bit. She states that she has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She notes that she has been less strict with her diet since the last heart attack and that she loves pizza. She states that she has been trying to watch her salt intake, but that it is difficult.
Medical Treatment: The patient will continue her lisinopril 20 mg daily.
3. Acute heart failure.
Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where we had to place another stent. The patient reports that she had been doing well since her last heart attack, but that she had been experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath. She states that she had been walking her dog every day, but that she had been noticing some leg swelling. The patient reports that she is able to lie flat at night, but that she has been having to sit up a little bit. She states that she has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She notes that she has been less strict with her diet since the last heart attack and that she loves pizza. She states that she has been trying to watch her salt intake, but that it is difficult.
Medical Treatment: The patient will take lasix 40 mg daily and aldactone 12.5 mg daily.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will return in 2 months for a repeat echo. }}<|eot_id|>
